# What Docker must not send to the daemon.
#
# The Dockerfile already copies by name, so nothing here is load-bearing for
# today's build. It is load-bearing for tomorrow's: the day somebody adds a
# `COPY . .`, this file is what decides whether a key goes with it. Two
# defences, because the one that fails is always the one there was only one of.
#
# It also keeps the context small, which is why a build does not spend its
# first seconds uploading __pycache__.

# Secrets. First, and with every variant spelled out -- .env.local is as
# sensitive as .env, and a pattern that misses it is worse than no pattern
# because it looks like coverage.
.env
.env.*
*.env
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
id_rsa*
secrets/

# Python build and cache artefacts. A .pyc compiled by a different interpreter
# on a different platform is at best dead weight and at worst confusing.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# A host virtualenv holds platform-specific binaries built for macOS; the
# image builds its own inside Linux.
.venv/
venv/

# Development-only inputs. The image installs from the lock file, and a test
# runner in a production image is surface for no benefit.
requirements-dev.txt
tests/
pyproject.toml

# Version control and editor state.
.git/
.gitignore
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
.DS_Store
